# clang-tidy runs the checks that find bugs. It runs none of the ones that have opinions about
# style, because style is already settled elsewhere and a second voice on it would only disagree:
# layout by .clang-format, includes and naming by CPPLINT.cfg, and the whole -Wall -Wextra
# -Wpedantic -Werror set by the compiler on every build. What clang-tidy adds over those is the
# path-sensitive analysis (clang-analyzer-*) none of them can do — following a value across
# branches to find the null dereference or the leak on the one path nobody tested.
#
# The enabled set is deliberately narrow, and it was chosen from a measurement rather than from
# taste. Run over five representative translation units, bugprone-* + clang-analyzer-* +
# concurrency-* + performance-* + misc-* reported 560 findings — of which 497 came from three
# misc-* checks alone: misc-include-cleaner (228, an include-what-you-use pass that disagrees with
# how this codebase groups headers), misc-non-private-member-variables-in-classes (178, which fires
# on every Config and every DTO, all of them public data by design), and misc-const-correctness
# (91). None of the three names a defect. A check that reports 89 things in one file trains the
# reader to skim the output, which costs more than the check is worth, so misc-* is off entirely.
#
# Four further checks are excluded by name, each because it is structurally wrong for this
# codebase rather than because its findings are inconvenient:
#   - bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters fires on adjacent same-typed parameters, and
#     (index, subindex) is the shape of most of the device API. Renaming cannot fix it and the
#     types cannot change.
#   - performance-enum-size wants a smaller underlying type on enums that cross the HTTP surface,
#     where the width is part of the contract and the saving is nothing.
#   - bugprone-unchecked-optional-access reported 207 findings over the tree, *every one of them in
#     a test and none in shipped code*. Its dataflow engine cannot see through gtest's ASSERT_
#     macros, so the mandated idiom — ASSERT_TRUE(r) << r.error(); then *r — reads to it as an
#     unchecked dereference. A check that is silent on the code it was meant to guard and noisy on
#     the code that already proves its own precondition is measuring the wrong thing here.
#   - bugprone-suspicious-missing-comma exists to catch a dropped comma in an array of string
#     literals. Every hit is a procedure descriptor whose prose clang-format wrapped at 100
#     columns, which is adjacent-literal concatenation working as intended.
#
# The fifth exclusion is different in kind: clang-analyzer-optin.core.EnumCastOutOfRange. It is a
# reasonable hygiene check — it is what flagged resolveSiiCategoryType forming a SiiCategoryType
# from a raw EEPROM word before knowing the word was an enumerator. Note what it did *not* find:
# that enum fixes its underlying type, so the cast was well defined and the code was correct; the
# rewrite states intent at a validator boundary and is not a bug fix. It is off because its only
# remaining reports are inside uWebSockets' TopicTree.h, which we cannot annotate, and because
# clang-tidy's header filters do **not** apply to static-analyzer diagnostics (verified: neither
# ExcludeHeaderFilterRegex nor --exclude-header-filter suppresses them, and the vcpkg include is
# already -isystem). Two unfixable reports would leave the target permanently red, which is worth
# more than a check with no defect to its name here. Do not expect UBSan's -fsanitize=enum to stand
# in for it either: for a fixed-underlying-type enum there is no undefined behaviour for UBSan to
# trap, so this coverage does not come back through the sanitizer plan in NEXTGEN.md.
#
# Checks that fire only a handful of times are deliberately left on even where the current hit is
# defensible — bugprone-command-processor on the one popen, bugprone-exception-escape on main.
# A finding worth answering at the call site (with a reason) is not a reason to blind the check.
#
# WarningsAsErrors is '*' so a finding fails the target, matching cppcheck's --error-exitcode=1.
# Suppressing is not the way out of a report here: fix it, or argue the check off this list.
Checks: >
  -*,
  bugprone-*,
  clang-analyzer-*,
  concurrency-*,
  performance-*,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -bugprone-suspicious-missing-comma,
  -bugprone-unchecked-optional-access,
  -clang-analyzer-optin.core.EnumCastOutOfRange,
  -performance-enum-size

# The compilation database is written by whichever compiler configured the build, and on Linux that
# is GCC. Four test targets add -Wno-array-bounds and -Wno-stringop-overflow to work around GCC
# false positives in libstdc++; clang does not know the second flag and rejects the translation
# unit over it, which cost 41 parse errors before this line existed. Ignoring an unknown -Wno- flag
# is exactly right for an analyser reading someone else's build.
ExtraArgs: ['-Wno-unknown-warning-option']

CheckOptions:
  # An explicit `(void)` cast is how C++ spells "I am ignoring this on purpose", and the tests use
  # it exactly that way — `(void)dm.init(...)` in a test whose subject is what happens *after* a
  # failed init. Without this the check cannot be satisfied except by inventing an unused variable,
  # which says less. A bare unignored call still reports.
  - key: bugprone-unused-return-value.AllowCastToVoid
    value: true

# Findings are reported for our own headers as well as our .cc files — a header-only bug in
# libs/api or a template in libs/core would otherwise be invisible, since clang-tidy only reports
# on the main file by default. The exclusion keeps vcpkg and the vendored SOEM out of the output:
# they are third-party, we do not fix them, and their findings would bury ours.
HeaderFilterRegex: '.*/(apps|libs)/.*'
ExcludeHeaderFilterRegex: '.*/(extern|build)/.*'

WarningsAsErrors: '*'
FormatStyle: file
